{%- comment -%}
  Applied filters, as removable chips above the results.

  Usage: {% render 'filter-chips', collection: collection %}

  The sidebar already had ticked boxes and a "Clear all", but on a phone the
  sidebar is a drawer — closed. So the one state a shopper most needs to see
  while reading the results, which filters are narrowing them, was the one
  piece of state only visible somewhere else. Two rows down from "12
  products" with no explanation of why it is not 300, the usual conclusion
  is that the shop is small rather than that a filter is on.

  Each chip removes exactly one value through Shopify's own url_to_remove,
  so nothing here has to understand the facet query format.

  ── Placement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Deliberately inside .shop-results. The collection page re-renders through
  the Section Rendering API and swaps three regions — .results-count,
  .shop-filters and .shop-results — so a chip row living inside the third
  one updates itself on every filter change with no extra wiring. Put it
  outside and it would go stale the moment a filter was applied, which is
  precisely when it is being read.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign coll = collection | default: collection

  # Is anything on? Checked before rendering so the row is absent rather than
  # empty when nothing is filtered — an empty "Filtered by" bar is noise.
  assign has_active = false
  for f in coll.filters
    if f.active_values.size > 0
      assign has_active = true
    endif
    if f.min_value.value != nil
      assign has_active = true
    endif
    if f.max_value.value != nil
      assign has_active = true
    endif
  endfor
-%}

{%- if has_active -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    aria-live, because on mobile the filter drawer stays open while values
    are ticked and the results swap behind it. Without this a screen-reader
    user gets no notification that anything changed at all.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="fchips" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Applied filters">
    <span class="fchips-label">Filtered by</span>

    {%- for f in coll.filters -%}
      {%- for value in f.active_values -%}
        <a class="fchip" href="{{ value.url_to_remove }}" rel="nofollow">
          <span class="fchip-k">{{ f.label }}:</span>
          <span class="fchip-v">{{ value.label }}</span>
          {%- comment -%}
            The × is decorative — the link's own text already says which
            filter this is, and "Remove" is carried by the accessible name
            below rather than repeated visually.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="mi fchip-x" aria-hidden="true">close</span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">— remove this filter</span>
        </a>
      {%- endfor -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        A price range is one chip, not two, because that is how it was set
        and how it is removed — Shopify hangs a single url_to_remove off the
        filter for both ends. Either end may be unset, so the label is built
        from whichever are present.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if f.type == 'price_range' -%}
        {%- assign has_price = false -%}
        {%- if f.min_value.value != nil -%}{%- assign has_price = true -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- if f.max_value.value != nil -%}{%- assign has_price = true -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- if has_price -%}
          <a class="fchip" href="{{ f.url_to_remove }}" rel="nofollow">
            <span class="fchip-k">{{ f.label }}:</span>
            <span class="fchip-v">
              {%- if f.min_value.value != nil -%}{{ f.min_value.value | money }}{%- else -%}{{ 0 | money }}{%- endif -%}
              &nbsp;–&nbsp;
              {%- if f.max_value.value != nil -%}{{ f.max_value.value | money }}{%- else -%}{{ f.range_max | money }}{%- endif -%}
            </span>
            <span class="mi fchip-x" aria-hidden="true">close</span>
            <span class="visually-hidden">— remove this filter</span>
          </a>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      sf-clear is the class the page's own click handler already intercepts
      for the sidebar's Clear all, so reusing it here gets the same in-place
      re-render for free rather than a full navigation.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="fchip-clear sf-clear" href="{{ coll.url }}" rel="nofollow">Clear all</a>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
